.TH MXP 3
.SH NAME
mxp \- ET-SoC-1 Maxion packet mailbox link to the PCIe host
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #E /dev

.B /dev/mxp
.B /dev/mxpctl
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On the
.I maxion
kernel of an Esperanto ET-SoC-1,
.B #E
is a raw IP point-to-point link to the PCIe host through one 4096-byte
page of service-processor mailbox SRAM.
It is the guest side of the
.B mxpacket
transport (ABI 1) of
.IR et-platform ;
the host side is the
.B et-soc1
driver loaded with
.BR "mx_packet=1 mx_packet_netdev=1" ,
whose
.B etmx
interface is the other end.
The page has no interrupt: a kernel process polls it,
every millisecond while there has been traffic in the last two seconds
or a packet is waiting, and every 10 ms otherwise.
.PP
The device exists only when the device tree describes a usable session,
as the host's
.B "mxpctl bootstrap"
writes it: exactly one enabled
.B packet@
node, compatible
.BR esperanto,maxion-packet-v1 ,
whose
.B reg
is one aligned page, and in
.BR /chosen ,
.BR esperanto,mxpacket-page-initialized ,
.B esperanto,mxpacket-abi
of 1,
a nonzero
.B esperanto,mxpacket-fw-id
and normally an
.BR rng-seed .
Otherwise the directory is empty and the page is never touched.
The guest boot identity is drawn from the kernel's random pool mixed
with the
.BR rng-seed .
After attaching, the page's firmware identity must equal the bootstrap's
.BR esperanto,mxpacket-fw-id .
.PP
Each read of
.B mxp
returns one received IPv4 or IPv6 packet; each write sends one,
of at most 1500 bytes.
Only whole packets whose IP header length agrees with the record
length are accepted, in either direction; others are counted and
dropped.
.B mxp
may be open only once; normally the IP stack holds it:
.IP
.EX
<>/net/ipifc/clone {
	echo bind netdev /dev/mxp >[1=0]
	echo add 192.0.2.2 255.255.255.255 192.0.2.1 1500 >[1=0]
}
.EE
.PP
which is what the
.I maxion
boot script does when
.B /dev/mxpctl
exists, with
.B $mxpip
and
.B $mxppeer
overriding the addresses.
.PP
The
.I maxioncpuwait
kernel is
.I maxion
with the cpuwait boot file system: a diskless cpu server whose rcpu
clients (drawterm
.BR -r )
supply the root of their sessions.
Its boot method
.B mxpcpuwait
.RI [ local
.RI [ peer ]]
configures this link as above (ipconfig cannot bind a netdev medium)
and then waits for clients on
.BR tcp!*!17019 ,
as the
.B cpuwait
method does.
Booted with
.BR "service=cpu nvram=/lib/nvram nobootprompt=mxpcpuwait" ,
it takes its keys from the well-known nvram of the cpuwait
servers (host owner glenda, authdom cpuwait, password
.BR glen||glenda ),
so the link must reach only trusted hosts.
.PP
Reading
.B mxpctl
returns the driver phase
.RB ( absent ,
.BR entropy ,
.BR attach ,
.BR run ,
.BR stopped ),
why it stopped, and the endpoint status: local and peer states,
fault reason, readiness, ring credit, sequence numbers, the
firmware, host and guest identities, the core's counters, and the
driver's own
.RB ( rxbad ,
.BR rxdrop ,
.BR txbad ,
.BR txdrop ,
.BR txexpired ).
Messages written to it:
.TP
.B quiesce
Publish QUIESCING and stop.
This is terminal, as is any fault: the session cannot be resumed or
re-attached until the card is cold-booted and armed again.
The host must still hold its device open to fence its own side.
.TP
.BI poll " active idle"
Set the two poll periods in milliseconds.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/ainekko/devmxp.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/ainekko/mxpcore.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ip (3)
.SH BUGS
The mailbox holds one full-size record each way, so every packet waits
for the peer's poll to return credit; throughput is a few megabits per
second.
There is no heartbeat: a peer that silently stops is not detected.
